(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-2-disorders-in-immunity-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Disorders in Immunity — Quiz 2","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How is allergy or hypersensitivity defined?","choices":["A loss or deficiency of immune function","An exaggerated, misdirected immune response to an allergen","A response using mechanisms unique to allergic disease","An abnormal response directed against self antigens"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the critical point is that it uses the SAME mechanisms as protective immune reactions — nothing novel is happening, it is simply happening against the wrong target. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which tissues are typically involved in immune complex disease?","choices":["Joint, skin and kidney","Lung and liver exclusively","Bone marrow and thymus","Brain and spinal cord"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the symptoms are DELAYED by hours to days, unlike the immediate reaction of type I. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Why does a food allergen produce urticaria in the skin?","choices":["Skin mast cells are sensitised by ingestion directly","Immune complexes deposit in dermal vessels","Antigen enters blood vessels and is carried to the skin","Mast cells migrate from the gut to the skin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is the clearest illustration that the organ of allergic expression need not be the portal of entry. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What are the three general methods of managing type I hypersensitivity?","choices":["Prevention, pharmacology, desensitisation","Antibiotics, antivirals and antifungals","Surgery, radiation and chemotherapy","Transfusion, transplantation and grafting"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and they escalate: avoid the allergen, blunt the response, or retrain the immune system against it. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which skin tests are used in allergy diagnosis?","choices":["Cross-matching against donor red cells","Skin prick testing and intradermal testing","Patch testing alone","Tuberculin testing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, both producing wheal and flare reactions. The patch test is separate and is used for contact dermatitis, a type IV reaction. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What happens on the sensitising dose in type I allergy?","choices":["T cells are recruited to the site of contact","B cells form IgE, which arms mast cells","Mast cells degranulate and symptoms appear","Immune complexes form and deposit in tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and generally there are no signs or symptoms at this stage — the patient is being armed without knowing it. Basophils are armed the same way. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which blood findings support an allergy diagnosis?","choices":["Specific IgE and increased basophils","Raised IgG against the allergen","Complement consumption","Positive blood culture"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and they complement the skin tests by measuring the sensitisation directly rather than through a provoked skin reaction. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What does cromolyn sodium do?","choices":["Reverses airway constriction directly","Blocks degranulation","Reduces IgE production by inhibiting lymphocytes","Binds to IgE as a monoclonal antibody"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it prevents the mast cell from releasing its granule contents rather than blocking the mediators afterwards. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What do basophils initiate?","choices":["A TH1 response and production of IgG","Complement activation by the classical pathway","Cytotoxic T cell proliferation","A TH2 response and production of IgE"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. They are rare granulocytes, and in normal defence they act against helminths — the same axis that produces allergy when misdirected. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which skin responses fall under type IV?","choices":["The tuberculin skin test","Wheal and flare reactions","Urticaria from ingested food","Angioedema"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with contact dermatitis arising from plants, metals and cosmetics. Graft rejection is the third category of type IV T cell response. Contact dermatitis too. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What immune response does helminth infection stimulate?","choices":["Complement-mediated lysis of the parasite","No adaptive response at all","CD4 TH2-like responses with high IgE","CD8 cytotoxic responses with high IgG"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, together with an increase in eosinophils and mast cells — exactly the profile of allergic disease, which is why the absence of allergy needs explaining. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which hypersensitivity types are B cell mediated?","choices":["Types I and II only","Type IV only","All four types","Types I, II and III"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and type IV is the exception — it is T cell mediated, which is why it is delayed rather than immediate. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"How many people are heavily and persistently infected with helminths?","choices":["About one billion","About one million","About ten thousand","Fewer than one hundred thousand"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, in countries where helminth infection is endemic — and notably those populations do not show corresponding allergic disease. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What distinguishes angioedema from urticaria?","choices":["A different antibody class is involved","It is T cell rather than antibody mediated","It is a more prolonged response","Mast cell activation deeper in the skin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The mechanism is identical; the depth at which it happens is what changes the appearance. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"How do injected allergens block the allergic response?","choices":["They destroy circulating IgE directly","They induce tolerance by deleting all B cells","They raise allergen-specific IgG","They deplete mast cells from the tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, so the mast cells do not degranulate — the allergen is intercepted before it can crosslink the cell-bound antibody. It blocks the allergen from reaching IgE. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"How do corticosteroids act in allergy?","choices":["They block mast cell degranulation","They bind histamine receptors on target organs","They block leukotriene synthesis","They inhibit lymphocytes to reduce IgE"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, acting upstream on the production of the antibody rather than on the mediators it releases. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What do the antibodies bind in type II hypersensitivity?","choices":["Nothing — no antibody is involved","Cell surface antigens","Soluble antigens, forming complexes","Antigen already bound to mast cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the consequence follows: complement is activated, with cell lysis by the membrane attack complex and phagocytosis. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"How does epinephrine act in anaphylaxis?","choices":["It reforms tight junctions and relaxes bronchi","It blocks histamine receptors on target organs","It blocks synthesis of leukotrienes","It is a monoclonal antibody against IgE"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it also prevents upper airway mucosal oedema, stimulates the heart, and binds receptors on immune cells to suppress histamine release. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which conditions are given as type II examples?","choices":["Hay fever and anaphylaxis","Transfusion reactions and Rh incompatibility","Serum sickness and the Arthus reaction","Contact dermatitis and graft rejection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, together with some autoimmune conditions — autoimmune haemolytic anaemia and myasthenia gravis, where antibodies target acetylcholine receptors. ABO incompatibility too. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What happens to the immune complexes in type III hypersensitivity?","choices":["They bind directly to mast cell IgE","They are destroyed by cytotoxic T cells","They embed in basement membranes","They are cleared without consequence"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and once trapped they incite a damaging inflammatory response through phagocytosis and complement activation. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What does FEV1 measure?","choices":["The diameter of a wheal on skin testing","Air forcibly exhaled in one second","Total lung capacity at full inspiration","The concentration of IgE in serum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the maximum volume, used to plot the immediate response at under an hour against the late phase response after six hours. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What does the hygiene hypothesis propose?","choices":["Exposure to allergens in childhood causes lifelong allergy","Hygiene practices directly damage epithelial barriers","Vaccination produces IgE against vaccine components","Underused immune systems are less successfully regulated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Better hygiene, vaccination and antibiotics eradicated helminth and other parasitic infections in Western Europe and North America, leaving the system without its usual training. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which are examples of type I hypersensitivity?","choices":["Transfusion reactions","Serum sickness and lupus","Contact dermatitis","Hay fever and hives"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and they range from trivial to fatal while sharing one mechanism: IgE crosslinking on mast cells releasing inflammatory mediators. Anaphylactic shock too. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is omalizumab?","choices":["A leukotriene synthesis blocker","A histamine receptor blocker","A mast cell stabiliser","A monoclonal antibody against IgE"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, so it removes the antibody that arms the mast cell rather than acting on the cell or its mediators. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is the Arthus reaction?","choices":["A delayed T cell response to an injected antigen","An immediate wheal and flare","Localised dermal injury from inflamed blood vessels","A systemic reaction to foreign animal protein"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a vasculitis, typically following a second injection of vaccine at a site, and normally self limiting. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which combination produces haemolytic disease of the newborn?","choices":["Two Rh negative parents","Any ABO mismatch between mother and fetus","An Rh negative mother with an Rh positive fetus","An Rh positive mother with an Rh negative fetus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the first pregnancy is normally fine because it is the sensitising event; subsequent pregnancies carry the haemolytic risk. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What is said about the relationship between the portal of entry and the organ affected?","choices":["They may or may not be the same","They are always the same","They are never the same","The portal determines the severity but not the site"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is why an ingested food allergen can produce urticaria in the skin — antigen entering blood vessels is carried there. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What are the consequences of a transfusion reaction?","choices":["Delayed T cell infiltration of the transfused tissue","Bronchial smooth muscle contraction","Red cell destruction, glomerular blockage, fever and jaundice","Immune complex deposition in joints and skin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and even where complement activation is incomplete, antibody-coated cells may attract natural killer cells or macrophages. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What characterises systemic anaphylaxis?","choices":["Immune complex deposition in joints and kidneys","Sudden respiratory and circulatory disruption","A localised wheal and flare skin reaction","A delayed reaction appearing after several days"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Allergen reaches the bloodstream and activates connective tissue mast cells associated with blood vessels throughout the body. It can be fatal in a few minutes. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What produces urticaria?","choices":["Mast cells in the skin releasing histamine","Immune complexes depositing in dermal vessels","Cytotoxic T cells attacking keratinocytes","Complement lysing skin cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, causing raised itchy swelling. Angioedema is the same process happening in DEEPER skin, and eczema is a more prolonged allergic response. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 16"}]};
